  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Cut Cymbidium in Guatemala by Holland Orchids</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The main international markets for tropical flowers are Europe and North America. One of the major variations of cut flowers sold in the North American markets are cymbidiums. It is predicted that the cymbidium is one of the tropical flowers that will have an increase of more than 10% in florist&apos;s usage.Cymbidium has relatively high consumer price approximately &#x20AC;17 per stem. A cymbidium flowers once a year and can produce flowers for approximately 15 to 20 years. Sales of cymbidiums are increasing in North America and Europe, but the production requires high investments, resulting in considerable (financial) risks.Currently, main cymbidium producers are the Netherlands, Italy, South Africa and New Zealand. Guatemala offers great logistic and production perspectives due to its location and climate, competitive production, low transportation costs, and good access to main target North American markets.The applicant Star Orchids (was W &amp; J Valstar) and local partner Flores Bohemia will establish a greenhouse in Tecpan Guatemala for the cultivation of cut cymbidium in seven colours. The joint venture will be the first in Guatemala cultivating cut cymbidium flowers using innovative techniques for light/shade manipulation and leaf temperature. State of the art sensors to be able to manipulate irrigation, fertilisation, temperature, light, and humidity will be applied too.</narrative>
  </description>
